# Documented commands: what the new gate reads, what it found, and what it refuses to judge

Date: 2026-10-04.  Gate: scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py
Fixtures: scripts/ci/test-check-evidence-commands.py
Ledger:   scripts/ci/evidence-commands-allowlist.tsv

Two earlier gates read prose. check-evidence-paths.py rejects a document that points at a
session-private scratch directory, and check-doc-path-refs.py rejects a pointer to a file
that is not in the repository. Neither reads a captured transcript, and neither reads a
command. So the one kind of pointer a maintainer is most likely to copy-paste, a command
line, was read by nothing. This log records what that cost and what the new gate does
about it.

== 1. The finding that started it

    $ sed -n '61p;202p' docs/verification/protocol-mirror-coverage-2026-10-03.log

    $ bash scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh                    # GUI protocol types are up to date
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh                 # GUI protocol types are up to date

Line 61 is the transcript of what was actually run. Line 202 sits under a heading whose
job is to tell a reader how to reproduce the result, and it hands a bash script to
Python, which answers SyntaxError. A reader who follows it concludes either that the
reproduction is broken or that the document is.

The same shape had already cost this repository a false measurement, recorded in
docs/ci-test-debt.md under "测量脚本把 .py 交给 bash 跑": a sweep ran
`python3 scripts/ci/check-versions.sh`, Python raised on a bash file, the exit code the
loop read came from `tail`, and the gate was reported as passing.

The line was fixed rather than recorded, because the fix is one word:

    $ git diff -U1 docs/verification/protocol-mirror-coverage-2026-10-03.log | tail -5

    @@ -201,3 +201,3 @@
         $ python3 scripts/ci/check-protocol-mirror.py --print     # 32 of 32 / 40 of 40
    -    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh                # GUI protocol types are up to date
    +    $ bash scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh                   # GUI protocol types are up to date

== 2. What is read

Every tracked *.md, plus every docs/verification/*.log. A command line is either a line
whose first non-space text is "$ ", or a line inside a fence labelled as a shell (sh,
bash, console, pwsh, ...), with the fence rules of CommonMark (a fence closes only on its
own marker and not on a shorter run).

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py --list | wc -l
    320

The counts inside those files were measured before the rules were set, to decide what the
rules could afford to be strict about:

    scanned files        : 320  (transcripts 45, markdown 275)
    $-marked lines       : 365   (transcripts 345, markdown 20)
    shell-fence lines    : 722   (transcripts 0, markdown 722)
    command lines total  : 1087

No transcript uses a labelled fence, so every fence line is in Markdown. The two shapes are
not interchangeable: a transcript marks its commands with `$` because it is a recording,
while a guide puts them in a fence because that is what renders. A gate reading only `$`
lines was the first version of this one, and it is the weaker version, measured in
section 4.

This log is itself in the scanned set, so the counts above include it, including the `$`
lines below. The numbers were written last, after the last edit to any scanned file,
because writing them earlier would have made them false.

== 3. Rule 1: an interpreter cannot read what it was handed

Where a command line runs a named interpreter over a script file, the script's extension
has to belong to that interpreter's family. python3 gets .py, bash gets .sh, pwsh gets
.ps1, node gets .mjs/.cjs/.js. The match is on the basename, so /usr/bin/python3 is the
same interpreter, and the extension is matched case-insensitively, so a .SH is a shell
script. Commands that name no script file (python3 -c, python3 -m, bash -c,
python3 - <<'PY') make no claim about a file and are not examined.

Across every command line in the repository the rule found exactly one mismatch, and it is
line 202 above. That line is fixed, so the finding left on the tree is the quotation of it
in section 1 of this log, which section 6 records:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py
    check-evidence-commands: 320 files, 1087 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live

The two counts before "recorded" are raw finding counts, so a tree can be green while both
are non-zero: what exit 0 promises is that every finding has a live row on the ledger. The
test that runs this repository checks exactly that, by re-reading the --all output, because
asserting "zero findings" here would have gone red the day the first quotation was
recorded.

== 4. Rule 2: a path a command names has to still be there

A transcript is a reproduction recipe, and a recipe naming a file that no longer exists
reproduces nothing. Four things are deliberately not claims that a file is present, and
each was measured rather than assumed, because a rule that buries its findings in noise
gets switched off:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'                                            # counts, see below
    ...
    as shipped                     : 5
    creators removed               : 5
    copy sources unchecked         : 5
    patterns removed               : 10
    redirect removed               : 6
    none of them                   : 11

Reading them one at a time:

    - creators (mkdir, touch, rm, rmdir, tee, truncate, mkfifo, ln): a line may write or
      delete the path it names, and `ln -s nowhere link` is a legal dangling symlink, so
      ln is exempt as a whole. On today's tree this rule removes nothing, which is said
      rather than glossed over; what it buys is that a future
      `mkdir -p docs/verify && touch docs/verify/x.log` stays quiet;
    - copy destinations. `cp`, `mv` and `install` write their last argument and read the
      ones before it, so `cp docs/gone.md docs/out.md` is still a claim about
      docs/gone.md and no longer a claim about docs/out.md. This rule also removes nothing
      on today's tree, and it closed a hole the first version had: `cp gone.md dest` read
      as fine;
    - a redirect target, because `> target/debug/out.tsv` names an output. Without it the
      rule reports 6 paths where it reports 5;
    - a token shaped like a pattern, which is the difference between 5 and 10. The trap
      here is that the test has to run on the whole whitespace token, because the path
      regex stops at "<", so scripts/ci/x-<hash>.py truncates to scripts/ci/x- and would
      otherwise be reported as a missing file. A first version made exactly that mistake.

The pattern rule has a cost that is not hidden by an exclusion: a template name with no
pattern character in it, `changelogs/X.Y.Z.md`, is an honest finding, because as written
the command does not run. The fix went into the document, not into the rule; section 7.

A path is only a claim about this repository if its first segment is a tracked top-level
entry. That is the same anchor rule check-doc-path-refs.py uses: target/, /tmp/...,
../other/ and a directory that exists only in some developer's checkout are not statements
about the repository.

Rule 2 was also measured against the narrower reading of "command line". Restricted to
`$` lines it reports 4 absent paths where the shipped rule reports 5, and the one it misses
sits in a fenced recipe in a document whose whole purpose is to be executed. That is the
argument for reading fences, and mutation M3 in section 9 is the version of the gate that
did not.

== 5. The five absent paths that are recorded

Every one is real, and every one is kept because deleting or rewriting the line would
falsify a transcript.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py --all

    check-evidence-commands: docs/verification/evidence-commands-2026-10-04.log:19: `python3 scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh` cannot work: python3 is a python interpreter and `.sh` is a shell script, run it with bash (quoted-command)
    check-evidence-commands: crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:468: `bin/verify.sh` names a file that is not in the repository, so this command line reproduces nothing (other-root)
    check-evidence-commands: docs/verification/doc-path-refs-2026-10-03.log:165: `third_party/nfsserve` names a file that is not in the repository, so this command line reproduces nothing (recorded-absent)
    check-evidence-commands: docs/verification/doc-path-refs-2026-10-03.log:198: `docs/release-process.md` names a file that is not in the repository, so this command line reproduces nothing (recorded-absent)
    check-evidence-commands: docs/verification/docker-gate-mirror-2026-10-03.log:426: `docs/verification/todo-open-items-2026-10-03.tsv` names a file that is not in the repository, so this command line reproduces nothing (historical)
    check-evidence-commands: docs/verification/maintenance-line-review-2026-10-04.log:337: `sync/recon/2026-10-03-2bdd1d6a6-2.md` names a file that is not in the repository, so this command line reproduces nothing (recorded-absent)
    check-evidence-commands: 320 files, 1087 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; 6 problem(s)

--all is the audit view and exits 1 by design: it prints the recorded findings as problems,
which is the only way to see what the ledger is holding. The default run prints only the
findings nobody has accounted for.

bin/verify.sh is a sample hook script that calls a script belonging to whoever reads the
user guide, not a path this repository ships. third_party/nfsserve and
docs/release-process.md are named by transcripts that print `ls` refusing them, which is
the finding itself. The dated .tsv was retired the day after the transcript was captured,
and the recon record was written by an accidental live run and deleted after its mode was
measured.

Categories are a closed set (historical, recorded-absent, other-root, quoted-command),
each row carries a reason of at least 20 characters, and a row whose finding has gone away
fails as stale, so the ledger cannot accumulate rows left behind by fixed lines:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py --allowlist /dev/null | tail -1
    check-evidence-commands: 320 files, 1087 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 0 recorded; 6 problem(s)

== 6. The row keyed on a command, for a command quoted as evidence

An absent path is recorded by naming the path. Rule 1 needs a different key, because a
document may have to print a broken command to show what was wrong with it, and there is no
path to point at: the file exists, the interpreter is the mistake. Section 1 does exactly
that, so the row quotes the command itself, which is what the finding prints:

    $ printf '%s\n' 'python3 scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh	quoted-command	the log quoting it is about this exact typo ...' >> scripts/ci/evidence-commands-allowlist.tsv

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py
    check-evidence-commands: 320 files, 1087 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live

That row did not exist at first, and the gate went red on its own log: a quotation of a
mistake is indistinguishable from a recommendation unless the document says which it is.

The example line above is itself a finding the gate made and then withdrew. Written as it
is, the line contains `python3` followed by `scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh`, which is
rule 1's shape, and the first version of the rule matched an interpreter name wherever it
appeared in a line, so this log was reported for the command it was appending to a ledger.
The docstring had always promised that an interpreter inside a quoted string is not
examined, so the code was changed to keep that promise: a token whose first character is a
quote is data, not a command word.  The cost is a command word written in quotes
(`'python3' x.py`), which no document here writes, and the case is pinned by a fixture and
by mutation M20.

Two more properties of the category are pinned by fixtures rather than by the tree, because
the tree has one row and it is green:

    - a quoted-command row excuses only a rule-1 finding, and a path row excuses only a
      rule-2 finding. Without that split one loose row silences both rules, which is
      mutation M18; the subtler version, where both rules share one liveness counter so a
      path row stays live because some rule-1 message happens to match it, is M19;
    - a quoted-command row whose command no longer appears anywhere fails as stale, the
      same way a path row does. So the row above disappears the day section 1 is rewritten
      to describe the typo instead of printing it.

== 7. Four placeholders in a recipe that were fixed instead of recorded

.agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md is a document meant to be
pasted. On the version before the fixes it produced four findings:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import importlib.util, subprocess
    from pathlib import Path
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("ev", "scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py")
    m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(m)
    repo = m.Repo(Path(".").resolve())
    PLAY = ".agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md"
    text = subprocess.run(["git", "show", f"HEAD:{PLAY}"], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
    for lineno, cmd in m.command_lines(text):
        for bare, where in m.check_paths(repo, PLAY, lineno, cmd):
            print(f"{where}: `{bare}` names a file that is not in the repository")
    PY

    check-evidence-commands: .agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md:59: `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/some_module.rs` names a file that is not in the repository
    check-evidence-commands: .agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md:91: `crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/foo.rs` names a file that is not in the repository
    check-evidence-commands: .agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md:106: `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/X.Y.Z.md` names a file that is not in the repository
    check-evidence-commands: .agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md:107: `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/X.Y.Z.json` names a file that is not in the repository

(The heredoc prints the findings without the `check-evidence-commands: ` prefix; it is
added here so the four lines match what the gate printed.)

Recording those would have been the wrong call: a recipe with an invented file name is not
evidence that was true once, it is a recipe that does not work. All four were replaced with
paths that exist, or with a variable that makes the placeholder visible:

    $ git diff -U0 .agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md | grep -E '^[-+][^-+]'

    -git checkout upstream/main -- crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/some_module.rs
    +git checkout upstream/main -- crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/codex/read_file/mod.rs
    -gh api repos/xai-org/grok-build/contents/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/foo.rs \
    +gh api repos/xai-org/grok-build/contents/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/git_info.rs \
    -git show upstream/main:crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/foo.rs | head
    +git show upstream/main:crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/git_info.rs | head
    -cp crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/X.Y.Z.md ~/.grok/CHANGELOG.md
    -cp crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/X.Y.Z.json ~/.grok/CHANGELOG.json
    +V=1.0.9   # 本次移植要发的版本
    +"crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/$V.md" ~/.grok/CHANGELOG.md
    +"crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/$V.json" ~/.grok/CHANGELOG.json

The two git replacements were chosen, not grabbed. The checkout example overwrites the
working tree, so it names a file that is byte-identical in HEAD and upstream, which makes a
copy-paste a no-op:

    $ git cat-file -e HEAD:crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/codex/read_file/mod.rs && echo in HEAD
    in HEAD
    $ git cat-file -e upstream/main:crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/codex/read_file/mod.rs && echo in upstream/main
    in upstream/main

The other example is a read-and-compare recipe, so it names a file that differs between the
two trees, which is the situation that section is about:

    $ git cat-file -e HEAD:crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/git_info.rs && git cat-file -e upstream/main:crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/git_info.rs && git diff --quiet upstream/main HEAD -- crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/git_info.rs; echo "differs: $?"
    differs: 1

The changelog copies are the interesting two. `changelogs/X.Y.Z.md` is not a pattern by the
rule's definition, because it holds no character from the pattern set, so it is a plain
path claim, and the honest reading is that the recipe cannot run as printed: no such file
exists and nothing substitutes for it. The rewrite binds a variable first, so the command
runs as written and the thing to fill in is visible:

    $ sed -n '106,108p' .agents/skills/chaos-upstream-sync/references/port-playbook.md

    V=1.0.9   # 本次移植要发的版本
    cp "crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/$V.md" ~/.grok/CHANGELOG.md
    cp "crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/changelogs/$V.json" ~/.grok/CHANGELOG.json

== 8. What is a command line, pinned from both sides

Whether a line is a command at all decides whether both rules apply to it, so the fence
rules are pinned rather than trusted. A prompt-marked line is a command even inside a fence
labelled `text`; a fence opened with `~~~` is not closed by ```` ``` ````; a 4-run fence is
not closed by a 3-run one.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-evidence-commands.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 19 tests in 1.446s

    OK

The fixture is a real `git init` repository with a real index, because the anchor rule reads
`git ls-files`: it contains a transcript, two documents, five scripts across four families,
one uppercase extension, and one directory that exists on disk while being gitignored, so a
path into it is not a claim. Its dirty transcript carries eight interpreter findings, one of
them indented and with its operand in quotes, plus one absent path.

Non-vacuity is asserted rather than assumed. The clean-fixture test checks the counters it
is about to be silent for (files read, command lines read, mismatch count), so a scanner
that stopped matching anything fails instead of looking green; and one test runs the gate
against this repository, re-reads its --all output, and requires that every finding it
counts has a key on the ledger.

== 9. Mutation matrix

One change at a time, watched by the fixture suite and by the real tree, restored with
shutil.copy2 and confirmed byte-identical with filecmp (shallow=False). The driver first
refuses to measure unless both the fixtures and the real tree are green.

    baseline: fixtures rc=0; real tree rc=0 :: check-evidence-commands: 320 files, 1087 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live

    KILLED  M1   family comparison dropped                  10 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M2   every fence is a shell fence               3 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M3   fence lines are not commands at all        4 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M4   creator commands are not special           3 tests,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M5   a redirect target counts as a claim        2 tests,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M6   a pattern is a path                        6 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M7   existence is assumed                       9 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M8   any first segment anchors a claim          6 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M9   stale ledger rows tolerated                3 tests,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M10  a reason is not required                    1 test,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M11  any category accepted                       1 test,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M12  recorded findings are always hidden        2 tests,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M13  shell quotes are part of the operand        1 test,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M14  interpreter matched on the whole path      2 tests,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M15  extension case is significant               1 test,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M16  copy sources count as created too           1 test,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M17  a quoted command excuses nothing           2 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M18  any row may excuse either rule             4 tests,  real tree rc=1
    KILLED  M19  liveness not judged per category            1 test,  real tree rc=0
    KILLED  M20  an interpreter inside quotes is a command  2 tests,  real tree rc=1

    restored tree: fixtures rc=0, real tree rc=0 :: check-evidence-commands: 320 files, 1087 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live

Four of these are worth naming. M3 ("fence lines are not commands at all") is the mutation
that represents the first version of this gate, which checked paths only on "$ " lines; it
turns the real tree red, which is the measurement saying the broadening was worth having.
M13 is the pin for the operand `"scripts/ci/x.sh"` in the fixture: without the quote strip,
the extension read off that token is `sh"` and the mismatch silently disappears. M16 is the
copyist rule: with `cp` treated as a writer of all its arguments, the fixture's
`cp docs/gone.md ...` stops being a finding. M20 is the reverse, the quoted-data rule above:
with it removed, the gate reports its own ledger-writing example.

A driver lesson, recorded because it hid every mutation for one run: unittest prints a
Traceback for every failed assertion, so "output contained a Traceback" classified every
mutation as a crash. The crash signal is a run that never reaches its `Ran N tests` summary
line.

== 10. Wired at both call sites with identical flags

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py | tail -1
    check-guard-wiring: OK (53 files in scripts/ci/, 52 reachable, 48 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-workflow-yaml.py | tail -1
    workflow yaml: 2 file(s), no unquoted scalar with a mapping separator

    $ bash -n scripts/verify-in-docker.sh && echo parses
    parses

The same two commands run in .github/workflows/ci.yml and in the gates=() table of
scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; check-guard-wiring.py compares both directions and the flag
values at the two call sites, which is the rule that caught a threshold drift between legs
on an earlier gate.

== 11. What the gate does not judge

    - heredoc bodies: python3 - <<'PY' claims nothing about a file, and the body is not a
      command line. A heredoc that writes into the repository is not examined;
    - whether the exit code a quoted pipeline printed matches the prose next to it. No
      scanner can check that without running the command, and that exact gap is how the
      `| tail -3` false positive in docs/ci-test-debt.md happened;
    - a command whose interpreter is a variable (`$PY x.py`). The mirror image is judged:
      an interpreter named outside quotes where it is only an argument
      (`echo run python3 scripts/ci/x.sh`) reads as a claim and is reported;
    - absolute paths outside this repository, and any relative path whose first segment is
      not tracked;
    - whether a command that runs does the thing the heading over it claims. Rule 1 catches
      only the case where the interpreter cannot parse what it was handed;
      `bash scripts/x.py` is caught, `python3 scripts/x.py --check-versions` quietly doing
      nothing is not;
    - a copy destination (section 4), which is the price of the creator rule and is paid
      knowingly: `cp gone.md dest` is silent, `cp src dest-gone` is not.

The gate reads itself: scripts/ci/*.py is not in the scanned set, so the path-like strings
in its own docstring are not findings. Its fixtures, on the other hand, are checked by the
same code path that checks this log, and this log is checked by the gate it documents.
